    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kabul lay on trade routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and was claimed in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>, Mauryans, Kushans, Ghaznavids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, and in 1747 it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> made it his headquarters in 1504 and began his conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> from it in 1526, and in 1776 Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> moved the capital of the Durrani Empire to Kabul from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>.</li><li>After 1978 the city passed through revolution, Soviet occupation, a civil war that devastated about 80 percent of it by 1996, Taliban rule, the United States-led invasion of 2001 and the Taliban's return in 2021.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> loved Kabul and was initially buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, but following his wish he was later buried in Kabul.</p></aside>
    </section>

