    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, composed between 2000 and 1500 BCE, refers to a settlement called Kubha</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">305 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> cedes Kabul Valley to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">870</span><span class="rf-text">Ya'qub bin Laith as-Saffar conquers Kabul from the Hindu Shahis</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1504</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> captures Kabul and makes it his headquarters</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1776</span><span class="rf-text">Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> moves the capital of the Durrani Empire to Kabul</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1842</span><span class="rf-text">British forces return to Kabul after the retreat to Jalalabad and demolish the main bazaar</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a> announces <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>'s independence in foreign affairs at the Eidgah Mosque</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1996</span><span class="rf-text">The Taliban seize Kabul</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="Xuanzang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xuanzang_w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kabul lies at an altitude of 1,791 metres in a narrow valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>, on the Kabul River, in the eastern half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. It is believed to be over 3,500 years old, though the origin of the city, who built it and when, is largely unknown. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, composed between 2000 and 1500 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avesta">Avesta</a> refer to the Kabul River and to a settlement called Kubha. The meaning of the name is unknown. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> the city was known as Kubha, and Greek authors of antiquity called it Kophen, Kophes or Koa; Ptolemy recorded it as Kabura, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> also called it Ortospana, "High Place". The Chinese monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> recorded it as Gaofu, which Alexander Cunningham linked to one of the five tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>.</p><p>According to legend, a lake once lay in Kabul with an "Island of Happiness" in its middle, where a family of musicians lived and which a king made accessible by a bridge (pul) of straw (kah); the name Kabul is said to have formed from the two words. The Concise Oxford Dictionary of World Place Names calls a derivation from the Arabic root qbl, "meeting" or "receiving", unlikely. It is not known when the name was first applied to the city. It came into prominence after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapisa">Kapisa</a> and other cities in the thirteenth century. The name was first applied to the river, and the area between the Hindu Kush and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a> was also known as Kabulistan.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Kabul&#8221; cites where it covers Toponymy and etymology, Antiquity:</p><ul><li>M. Hassan Kakar, Kabul (Oxford University Press, 2008)</li><li>John Everett-Heath (ed.), Kabul (Oxford University Press, 2020)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> Kabul became a centre of learning for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and Hinduism, and an inscription on the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> lists it among the 29 countries of the empire. Under the Mauryans trade flourished through the use of uniform weights and measures, public irrigation raised the harvest, and people worked as artisans, jewellers and carpenters. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> rulers patronised Buddhism, and the majority of the region's people followed it. In the late 14th century Kabul became a major trading centre in the kingdom of Timur.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, who ruled it for about 200 years, Kabul became a prosperous urban centre with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> such as the Char Chatta, and a mint that struck gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins until the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamgir-ii">Alamgir II</a>. It served as a military base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>'s campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> and Badakhshan, and the population was about 60,000. By the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> it had lost its metropolitan status, and an estimate puts its people at about 10,000 in 1700. Timur Shah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani">Zaman Shah</a> built religious and public buildings and settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a>, jurists and literary families with land grants and stipends. The population was estimated at 65,000 in 1878, 120,000 in 1940 and about 500,000 in 1979. The built-up area reached 68 square kilometres by 1962, almost fourteen times that of 1925.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sabuktigin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sabuktigin.jpg" alt="Sabuktigin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sabuktigin">Sabuktigin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Ghaznavid_amir_Sabuktigin,_citing_the_Samanid_amir_Nuh_II_as_his_overlord._Date_unknown,_minted_at_Ghazna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kabul Valley was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/median-empire">Median Empire</a> and then, from 549 BCE, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. After Alexander and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, the Seleucid Empire ceded it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> in 305 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a> took it in the early 2nd century BCE and lost it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a> around the middle of that century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a> expelled the Indo-Greeks by the mid 1st century BCE and lost the city to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> in the first century CE. Around 230 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> defeated the Kushans and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushano-sasanian-kingdom">Kushano-Sasanian Kingdom</a>. The Kidarites drove them out in 420, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> replaced the Kidarites in the 460s, and the Tibetan Empire briefly held the city between 801 and 815.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a> reached modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in 642, when Kabul was independent under the Kabul Shahis. Ya'qub bin Laith as-Saffar took the city from the Hindu Shahis in 870, and it reverted to Shahi rule around 900. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabuktigin">Sabuktigin</a> took it permanently around 977. The Ghaznavids, Ghurids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshahs</a> and Qarlughids followed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> invaded in the 13th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> captured Kabul in 1504 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> held it for the next 200 years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> took it in 1738, and in 1747 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> asserted Pashtun rule. Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> to Kabul in 1776.</p><p>In 1826 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> claimed the kingdom. The British reinstalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-shuja-durrani">Shah Shuja Durrani</a> in 1839,, and Akbar Khan took the throne from 1842 to 1845. Habibullah Khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a>, Nader Khan and Zahir Shah, the last king of Afghanistan, ruled in the 20th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/babur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babur.jpg" alt="Babur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humayun_and_Babur_(Late_Shah_Jahan_Album)_Babur_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">c.1650, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kabul has historically been the most ethnically diverse city in the country, and Dari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a> are the main languages, with Dari long the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> described eleven or twelve tongues spoken in the Kabul country, among them Arabic, Persian, Turkic, Mongolian, Hindi and Afghani, and tribes of Turks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarts">Sarts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazaras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>. A National Geographic article of 2003 put the city's people at 45 percent Tajik, 25 percent Pashtun and 25 percent Hazara. About 90 percent follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> and 10 percent are Shias, mostly Hazaras in the Dashte Barchi area. Under the Kushans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was widely followed, and Istakhri in 921 described a castle with Muslims in it and a town with unbelievers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Records of religious correspondence show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in Kabul since the 8th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">al-Idrisi</a> wrote of a Jewish quarter in the 12th century. Most of the roughly 2,000 who remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> left after 1979, and none are believed to remain.</p><p>Houses were generally walled compounds built around courtyards, with narrow passageways. The Kabul River divides the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a>. In the 20th century the city modernised under Habibullah Khan, with electricity, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> and a postal service, and the first modern high school, Habibia, was founded in 1903. Amani and Lycee Esteqlal were founded in the 1920s, and Kabul University opened in 1932. The city had leather and textile industries by 1916. In the second half of the century it became a stop on the hippie trail and was nicknamed the "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mongol invasion of the 13th century caused major destruction in the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapisa">Kapisa</a> and other cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, visiting in 1333, found Kabul formerly a vast town whose site was occupied by a village inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war">First Anglo-Afghan War</a> a local uprising in 1841 killed the British resident, and in the retreat from Kabul to Jalalabad in 1842 Afghan tribesmen killed 4,500 regular British troops and 14,000 civilians. The British returned later that year and demolished the main bazaar in revenge. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war">Second Anglo-Afghan War</a>, which broke out in 1879, the Bala Hissar was partly destroyed by a fire and an explosion.</p><p>On 28 April 1978 President Daoud and most of his family were killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saur-revolution">Saur Revolution</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> invaded on 24 December 1979 and heavily occupied Kabul. A major uprising, the 3 Hut uprising, broke out in February 1980 and led to a night curfew that remained for seven years. The city's population rose from around 500,000 in 1978 to 1.5 million in 1988, largely from internal refugees. After the fall of Najibullah's government in April 1992 mujahideen factions entered the city, and shelling by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulbuddin-hekmatyar">Gulbuddin Hekmatyar</a>'s party escalated into the "Kabul Wars", in which at least 30,000 civilians were killed. About 80 percent of the city was destroyed by 1996.</p><p>The Taliban seized Kabul on 27 September 1996. In November 2001 the Northern Alliance captured the city after the Taliban left it, following an American invasion. The Taliban took it again on 15 August 2021.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan.jpg" alt="Amanullah Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Amanullah_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>'s tomb lies in the Gardens of Babur, the Bagh-e Babur, with its Persian couplet, "If there is a paradise on earth, it is this, and it is this, and it is this!" The gardens were rebuilt in 2005. Babur's writing on Kabul and the descriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, Istakhri and George Forster, who called 18th-century Kabul "the best and cleanest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-roman-province">Asia</a>", give a record of the city across centuries. Its buildings reflect its links with many empires. The Buddhist Chakari minaret was likely built in the Kushan era. The Mausoleum of Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> is octagonal, and the Eidgah Mosque is a fine example of the Babur era. The Bagh-e Bala Palace was designed in a mixed Mughal and British Indian style, and the Darul Aman Palace, begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a>, is the best known example of modern Western design.</p><p>The Kabul River still divides the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a>, though since the 21st century it has run dry most of the year. The old centre holds Bala Hisar, Deh Afghanan and Murad Khane. After 2001 many damaged landmarks were rebuilt or restored, including the Mahmoud Khan Bridge clock tower in 2013 and the Taj Beg Palace in 2021. Kabul is today the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, with an estimated population of about 5.3 million, and Dari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a> are spoken by most of its people.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kabul lies high in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and is believed to be over 3,500 years old. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> made it his headquarters in 1504, and Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> made it the capital in 1776. It is now the capital and largest city of Afghanistan.</em></p>

